    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that prism thing makes sense, thanks. but how does the math actually do that though? if i have a signal that's just one messy wave, how does the integral actually \"pick out\" the specific frequencies? it feels like there's some hidden mechanism at work or something.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The integral works by correlating your messy signal with many different reference sine and cosine waves simultaneously. When a reference frequency matches a component in your signal perfectly, they align to produce a large result, causing the other parts of the signal to cancel each other out due to their opposing phases. This act of multiplication and integration is essentially a continuous test where the math isolates the specific frequency you are currently looking at. Over mathematically continuous intervals, these isolated contributions build up to reconstruct exactly which frequencies compose your original signal.",
      "source": "target",
